    slug: "ski-lodge-timber-frame-engineering",
    title: "How Is Timber Frame Engineering Done for a Ski Lodge?",
    description: "Ski lodge timber frame engineering covers heavy-timber post-and-beam design, connection engineering for snow and seismic loads, char-rate fire performance, and mountain-town design review.",
    h1: "How Is Timber Frame Engineering Done for a Ski Lodge?",
    answer: "The engineering answer is that a ski lodge's timber frame is a real structure wearing a romantic costume: the great-room trusses spanning forty feet over the lobby carry the heaviest snow loads in the building code, the connections must resolve those loads through wood joinery or steel hardware without splitting the timbers, and the whole frame has to stand up in a seismic event while looking like it was hewn a century ago. Direct answer: the engineer designs the heavy-timber frame for the full alpine snow and wind loads, engineers every connection for the actual forces with shrinkage and movement accounted for, uses heavy timber's inherent char-rate fire performance in the code analysis, and details the lateral system so the beautiful frame is also the building's seismic backbone.\n\nHeavy timber is a code-recognized construction type with real structural rules. The large cross-sections — posts and beams typically 8 inches and up in the smallest dimension — give the members the capacity to carry long spans and heavy snow, and the engineer sizes them for bending, shear, and deflection under the jurisdiction's adopted ground snow load, including the drift and unbalanced cases at the lodge's dormers, clerestories, and roof steps. Long-span trusses over the great room are engineered as true trusses: the engineer computes the axial force in every chord and web member, designs the heel and peak joints for those forces, and checks deflection against the finishes below — a truss that sags visibly under snow load ruins the architecture it was meant to ennoble.\n\nConnections are where timber frames succeed or fail. Wood shrinks across the grain as it dries — a twelve-inch beam can lose a meaningful fraction of an inch — so the engineer details connections that accommodate movement: slotted bolt holes, shrinkage-compensating hardware, and steel knife plates or concealed hangers where the architecture demands clean lines. Every connection is designed for the actual load path: the tension splice in a bottom chord, the shear at a beam seat, the uplift at a post base in a wind event. The engineer specifies the bolt grades, plate thicknesses, and bearing lengths on the drawings, because a timber connection built from a generic detail is a guess, and guesses fail under snow load.\n\nFire performance is heavy timber's quiet advantage. Large wood members char at a slow, predictable rate, and the code recognizes this: the engineer uses the char-rate method to show that the remaining uncharred cross-section still carries the design loads after the required fire duration. This is what lets the timber stay exposed — no drywall wrap, no intumescent paint — while satisfying the fire-resistance requirements for the occupancy. The engineer coordinates the char analysis with the architect's exposed-wood intent early, because a member that needs another two inches of section for the fire case is cheap to upsize on paper and expensive to discover at plan check.\n\nThe lateral system hides inside the beauty. Timber frames are heavy and flexible, and a ski lodge in the Rockies or the Sierra sits in real seismic country — so the engineer designs the lateral resistance explicitly: diagonal bracing in selected bents, moment-resisting connections where the architecture allows, or concealed steel braced frames within timber-clad walls. The engineer checks drift so the frame's movement does not crack the stone chimney masses and glass walls the lodge aesthetic demands, and details the hold-downs and base connections for the full overturning. In towns like Aspen, Telluride, or Jackson, the design review board will judge the architecture; the engineer makes sure the structure behind it earns the approval too, with a submittal that shows the timber as an engineered system rather than a decorative one.",
    directAnswer: "Ski lodge timber frame engineering designs heavy-timber members and long-span trusses for full alpine snow loads, engineers every connection for real forces with wood shrinkage accounted for, uses char-rate fire analysis to keep the timber exposed, and builds the seismic lateral system into the frame itself.",
