        body: "The engineer works the deck as a snow structure first. Ground snow load from the jurisdiction's adoption, drift surcharges at parapets and penthouses, and the unbalanced case on any sloped ramp covers all feed the structural model; the engineer then checks the serviceability that keeps a garage working — deflection under the combined vehicle-plus-snow load, vibration of the long spans, and the drainage slopes holding true under load. Post-tensioned decks get the tendon layout coordinated with every drain, sleeve, and future EV conduit, because coring a post-tensioned deck after the fact is a structural emergency. Vehicle barriers at the open edges are engineered for the impact loads with their anchorage into the deck designed, not assumed.\n\nDurability detailing is where mountain garages are won or lost. The engineer specifies the concrete mix — air entrainment, water-cement ratio, permeability limits — and the reinforcement protection as a system matched to the deicing exposure, then details the joints, the waterproofing membranes over enclosed space, and the sealant maintenance the owner must perform. The specifications include a maintenance manual section: reseal joints on schedule, wash the decks in spring to remove chlorides, inspect the membranes. The engineer is blunt about this in the owner meetings — a garage's fifty-year life is a maintenance contract, and the design assumes the owner signs it.",
