        body: "Every process utility is recalculated for the new batch size and the elevation. Glycol loops are sized for the coldest crash-cooling demand with the warmest entering-fluid temperature the chiller will actually see at altitude; compressed air is sized for simultaneous kegging, pneumatic valves, and blow-off with a dryer that performs in cold intake air; and steam or hot-water systems are checked against the derated burner output, not the catalog rating. The engineer also plans utility routing so the expansion can tie into the existing plant without shutting it down for weeks — valved tie-in points, temporary bypasses, and a commissioning sequence that brings new fermenters online one at a time.\n\nAltitude touches more than burners. Fan and pump performance shifts with air density, refrigeration condensing temperatures run higher on hot summer days at elevation, and pressure-relief and vacuum devices on tanks must be verified for the local atmospheric pressure. The engineer documents each derate in the equipment schedules so the plan reviewer sees altitude accounted for rather than assumed away.",
