        question: "Why do retail plazas need grease rough-ins if the tenants are shops?",
        answer: "Because tenants change. A ski shop becomes a restaurant, and retrofitting grease waste through a finished slab and a terraced site costs an order of magnitude more than capping a rough-in during construction. The engineer provides each bay with a capped grease-waste connection, electrical capacity, and ventilation paths sized for a future food tenant, so the plaza adapts without structural surgery.",
